1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,919
Platform boundaries can now be declared.
That does not shrink the data inside a
feature. Arrays still give us fixed
length; real carts, search hits, and
timelines rarely promise one.

2
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:22,439
Suppose you are collecting tags a user
adds to a post. They might add three, then
delete one, then add five more. With an
array you allocate, copy, allocate again.

3
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:34,320
The natural question becomes: is there a
resizable, ordered collection that still
lets me talk about "the first item" and
"insert here"? That collection is a List.
A list is ordered and allows duplicates.

4
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:42,599
Position matters. The same value can
appear twice and mean two entries. Look at
this java for a moment. Walk the lines.

5
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:58,679
We program to the List interface and
construct an ArrayList — the default
workhorse for most application code.
add("a") appends. add(0, "b") inserts at
the front, shifting what was there.

6
00:00:58,799 --> 00:01:07,280
get(0) reads by index and prints b. The
list now holds [b, a]. Why ArrayList by
default?

7
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:17,019
Under the hood it uses a resizable array:
fast random access by index, fast append
at the end amortized, slower inserts at
the front because elements shift.

8
00:01:17,139 --> 00:01:31,299
That profile matches how most business
code actually uses lists. What about
LinkedList? It is a doubly linked
structure. Inserts and removals at known
nodes can be cheap, but getting the nth
element means walking links.

9
00:01:31,419 --> 00:01:38,619
In practice, LinkedList rarely wins for
typical access patterns. Prefer the boring
default until a profiler argues otherwise.

10
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:50,419
Programming to the List interface matters:
callers depend on list behavior, not on a
concrete class. Look at this java for a
moment. printAll does not care how the
list is stored.

11
00:01:50,539 --> 00:02:02,379
It cares that iteration and order behave
like a list. Now the failure mode that
bites during iteration: structural
modification while an iterator is live.
Look at this java for a moment.

12
00:02:02,499 --> 00:02:10,819
The enhanced for-loop uses an iterator.
Removing directly from the list during
that iteration can throw
ConcurrentModificationException.

13
00:02:10,939 --> 00:02:21,939
The safe habits are: use the iterator's
own remove, collect removals and apply
them after, or use removeIf. What if we
skip lists and keep resizing arrays by
hand?

14
00:02:22,119 --> 00:02:32,279
It works until the copy logic drifts, or
someone forgets to update a length
variable. Lists exist so resizable ordered
sequences stop being a DIY project.

15
00:02:32,399 --> 00:02:48,119
A small product-shaped fragment ties it
together: Look at this java for a moment.
The cart is ordered. Duplicate cables are
two lines until we remove them. That is
list thinking: sequence, not uniqueness,
not key lookup.

16
00:02:48,239 --> 00:02:57,039
Also watch aliasing: subList views share
structure with the backing list. When you
need a true independent snapshot, copy
into a new ArrayList.

17
00:02:57,159 --> 00:03:09,879
Prefer List.of and List.copyOf when the
collection should not grow — fixed lists
fail fast on add, which is better than a
mutable list that callers mutate by
accident. Growing sequences outgrew
arrays.

18
00:03:09,999 --> 00:03:22,799
List answered with ordered, duplicate-
friendly collections. ArrayList became the
default workhorse. Programming to List
kept APIs flexible. Iteration-modification
rules kept us from surprising exceptions.

19
00:03:22,919 --> 00:03:37,359
But sometimes duplicates are exactly what
you do not want. A set of roles, a set of
visited ids, a set of tags where "java"
should appear once. How does Java say
"unique membership"? That question opens
sets.
